1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,990
I love games. Could I make a living making them?

2
00:00:03,050 --> 00:00:06,850
It is okay if you have not decided whether to become
a designer or programmer.

3
00:00:06,990 --> 00:00:10,650
For people who make games, a love of games may have
been the starting point, too.

4
00:00:11,010 --> 00:00:14,910
But playing a lot of games and being good at making
them are different things.

5
00:00:14,970 --> 00:00:18,410
As a player, you open a chest and collect the item.
That is it.

6
00:00:18,450 --> 00:00:21,690
As a developer, you first have to figure out why the
chest will not open.

7
00:00:22,030 --> 00:00:25,370
We came looking for treasure and found four kinds of
work instead.

8
00:00:25,410 --> 00:00:31,150
Today we will examine one chest through game design,
programming, 2D art, and 3D art.

9
00:00:31,350 --> 00:00:37,290
Rather than polishing a portfolio, we will focus on
the work itself and the fundamentals behind it.

10
00:00:38,183 --> 00:00:40,243
First, watch this chest being opened.

11
00:00:40,283 --> 00:00:44,043
Someone considers where to place it so the player
will find it.

12
00:00:44,203 --> 00:00:47,583
Someone builds the feature that checks for a key and
adds the item.

13
00:00:47,783 --> 00:00:51,983
Others develop its appearance in drawings, then
build the model and its opening lid.

14
00:00:52,023 --> 00:00:56,683
The result is one chest, but making it means
answering different questions.

15
00:00:56,783 --> 00:01:00,683
In an actual studio, responsibilities depend on team
size and genre.

16
00:01:00,723 --> 00:01:05,763
Even within art, characters, environments,
interfaces, and animation may be separate
specialties.

17
00:01:05,803 --> 00:01:11,723
These four groups are a beginner's map, not a
checklist that everyone must master.

18
00:01:12,683 --> 00:01:16,263
Game designers do more than come up with interesting
ideas.

19
00:01:16,503 --> 00:01:20,243
They clarify those ideas so the team can understand
and build the same thing.

20
00:01:20,363 --> 00:01:24,423
Suppose you write: opening the chest gives a good
reward. Sounds finished, right?

21
00:01:24,823 --> 00:01:29,403
But what makes a reward good? Can you open it at any
time? Is the key consumed?

22
00:01:29,443 --> 00:01:33,543
What happens if the inventory is full, or the player
clicks an opened chest again?

23
00:01:33,643 --> 00:01:38,103
Separating these conditions, outcomes, and
exceptions is a foundation of design documentation.

24
00:01:38,263 --> 00:01:42,643
A pretty document matters less than making sure its
readers do not build different games.

25
00:01:42,683 --> 00:01:47,663
Start by describing one feature with sentences, a
table, and a simple flowchart.

26
00:01:47,843 --> 00:01:51,283
If you work with rewards and progression, practice
ratios,

27
00:01:51,323 --> 00:01:54,083
probability, and averages in a spreadsheet.

28
00:01:54,263 --> 00:01:57,983
Then watch people play. Do they find the chest where
you

29
00:01:58,023 --> 00:02:00,683
expected? Does the reward make them want to
continue?

30
00:02:00,863 --> 00:02:03,783
A design document is not a way to freeze an idea. It

31
00:02:03,823 --> 00:02:05,963
is a shared reference for building and improving it.

32
00:02:06,867 --> 00:02:11,607
Programmers make the computer handle the designed
rules consistently.

33
00:02:11,727 --> 00:02:16,327
We will use basic C++ as one example of a starting
point.

34
00:02:16,367 --> 00:02:20,487
Is the chest open? How many keys are left? Where
does the item go?

35
00:02:20,927 --> 00:02:23,847
Variables and data types represent those states.

36
00:02:24,187 --> 00:02:29,047
Conditions decide whether opening is allowed, while
loops and functions organize the work.

37
00:02:29,087 --> 00:02:34,307
At first, focus less on typing lots of code and more
on explaining what changes after each line.

38
00:02:34,347 --> 00:02:39,687
Later, learn references, pointers, and when objects
are created and destroyed.

39
00:02:39,787 --> 00:02:42,707
Continuing to refer to something that no longer
exists can cause trouble.

40
00:02:42,787 --> 00:02:45,567
When learning resource management, study not just
manual

41
00:02:45,607 --> 00:02:49,967
cleanup but safe cleanup tied to an object's
lifetime.

42
00:02:50,227 --> 00:02:53,647
Not every game uses the same language. The
environment may

43
00:02:53,687 --> 00:02:57,427
change, but reading data and control flow remains
useful.

44
00:02:58,317 --> 00:03:01,717
Now let us add the item from the chest to an
inventory.

45
00:03:01,877 --> 00:03:06,157
With only a few items, searching from the beginning
may seem fine.

46
00:03:06,397 --> 00:03:10,157
But what if there are many more items, and you run
that search frequently?

47
00:03:10,357 --> 00:03:16,837
Data structures concern how data is organized;
algorithms concern the steps used to process it.

48
00:03:17,137 --> 00:03:22,557
Compare arrays or vectors, queues, and hash tables
using small examples.

49
00:03:22,597 --> 00:03:25,097
No single structure is always best. The choice
depends

50
00:03:25,137 --> 00:03:27,177
on frequent operations and ordering requirements.

51
00:03:27,217 --> 00:03:32,237
As you implement searching and sorting, examine how
the amount of work grows with the data.

52
00:03:32,417 --> 00:03:37,117
If clicking the chest twice gives two rewards, it is
time to debug.

53
00:03:37,277 --> 00:03:42,377
Reproduce the symptom, set a breakpoint, and inspect
the chest state and item count.

54
00:03:42,417 --> 00:03:47,397
After fixing it, test repeated clicks and a full
inventory as well as the normal case.

55
00:03:47,457 --> 00:03:52,357
Making it work should also include being able to
find and fix the reason when it does not.

56
00:03:53,680 --> 00:03:57,233
In 2D art, start by considering how the chest should
look.

57
00:03:57,293 --> 00:04:02,913
Decorative details are tempting, but an oddly
oversized lid will stand out first.

58
00:04:03,133 --> 00:04:06,373
The foundation is observing an object and breaking
it into large forms.

59
00:04:06,493 --> 00:04:10,473
This chest can be simplified into a large box and a
thin lid.

60
00:04:10,693 --> 00:04:15,273
Establish their size and direction, then use
perspective to clarify which faces we see.

61
00:04:15,533 --> 00:04:19,053
Next, separate light and dark faces according to the
light direction.

62
00:04:19,253 --> 00:04:22,513
Before adding fine patterns, check whether the form
reads in grayscale.

63
00:04:22,553 --> 00:04:26,453
Finally, reduce it to its actual game size.
Something beautiful

64
00:04:26,493 --> 00:04:30,073
up close may look like a rock at a distance.

65
00:04:30,313 --> 00:04:36,433
For characters, develop anatomy and gesture; for
environments, develop space and composition.

66
00:04:36,513 --> 00:04:41,913
Learn both how to use the tool and how to judge what
to draw.

67
00:04:43,090 --> 00:04:47,557
3D art turns that form into a model and prepares it
for use in a game.

68
00:04:47,877 --> 00:04:53,857
A good front view is not enough. Proportions should
also work from the side and back.

69
00:04:54,417 --> 00:04:58,417
Begin with large masses, then organize the mesh you
need.

70
00:04:58,897 --> 00:05:01,857
More or fewer polygons is not automatically better.
Consider

71
00:05:01,897 --> 00:05:04,617
the silhouette, movement, and performance
requirements.

72
00:05:05,217 --> 00:05:08,337
Learn the different roles of UVs, which unfold the
surface, textures, which

73
00:05:08,377 --> 00:05:10,757
store surface information, and materials, which
respond to light.

74
00:05:10,897 --> 00:05:16,037
The names may sound complicated together, but
applying them to one chest makes their roles
clearer.

75
00:05:16,277 --> 00:05:21,477
Now watch this lid. A pivot in the middle does not
produce the opening motion we intended.

76
00:05:21,577 --> 00:05:25,557
Move the pivot to the hinge and check the model's
size and orientation for the game.

77
00:05:25,597 --> 00:05:30,417
This does not mean every 3D artist must draw like a
professional illustrator.

78
00:05:30,737 --> 00:05:35,397
What matters is observing form and judging what
looks wrong in space.

79
00:05:36,300 --> 00:05:38,960
Now bring everyone's work into one scene.

80
00:05:39,100 --> 00:05:43,460
The art looks good and the feature works, but the
player walks past the chest without noticing it.

81
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:46,780
That alone is not a reason to blame one person's
ability.

82
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:52,160
Examine the placement, contrast with the background,
and size in the camera view together.

83
00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:56,320
The designer revisits placement and rewards, the
artist checks form

84
00:05:56,360 --> 00:05:59,380
and contrast, and the programmer adjusts the
required behavior.

85
00:05:59,580 --> 00:06:02,720
Observation and communication are shared
fundamentals here.

86
00:06:02,760 --> 00:06:05,640
Showing what changes under which conditions is more

87
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:08,040
useful than simply saying that something is wrong.

88
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:12,260
Recording what changed and why makes it easier for
someone else to continue the work.

89
00:06:12,360 --> 00:06:17,740
These questions do not disappear in solo
development. You solve them by switching hats
yourself.

90
00:06:18,020 --> 00:06:20,820
Finishing a small feature means more than getting it
to appear

91
00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:24,040
once. It means checking that it works as intended.

92
00:06:24,967 --> 00:06:29,427
If you want to make a living making games, do not
try to master every field at once.

93
00:06:29,587 --> 00:06:33,687
Curious about design? Describe the rules of one
familiar feature on a single page.

94
00:06:33,827 --> 00:06:39,587
Curious about programming? Build a small inventory
and explain how storage and searching work.

95
00:06:39,747 --> 00:06:44,987
Curious about art? Observe an everyday object as
simple forms, then draw or model it.

96
00:06:45,147 --> 00:06:49,087
When you get stuck, return to the fundamentals you
need and apply what you learn.

97
00:06:49,127 --> 00:06:52,187
Avoid both waiting to finish all the basics before
making

98
00:06:52,227 --> 00:06:55,827
anything and skipping them by constantly switching
tools.

99
00:06:56,067 --> 00:06:59,987
You can work at a studio, in a small team, or on
your own.

100
00:07:00,027 --> 00:07:04,107
Making one game does not guarantee a job or an
income on any of those paths.

101
00:07:04,147 --> 00:07:09,747
But trying the work makes it clearer what you enjoy
and what you need to learn next.

102
00:07:09,867 --> 00:07:13,607
Take one step beyond the games you love. This time,
try being the person who makes them.
